window.questionData = [{"slug":"module-1","label":"Module I","title":"Marketing + strategic planning","filename":"Module1_Marketing_Study_Notes.txt","application":"Application Question: Eternal Eternal is the listed company that owns Zomato, Blinkit, Hyperpure and District. Food delivery is a mature two-player market in which it leads and margins have hardened. Blinkit, acquired in 2022 when quick commerce was widely considered unprofitable by design, grew to generate the clear majority of group revenue by the 2026 financial year and reached profitability at the segment level while its nearest rival was still losing several hundred crore rupees a quarter. Hyperpure supplies restaurants, many of them partners on the firm's own platform. District, the going-out and ticketing business, competes in a growing market in which the firm is not the leader. The firm renamed itself from Zomato to Eternal in 2025. Using the concepts in these notes, prepare an answer to the following. (a) State Eternal's business mission twice, once myopically in terms of the products it sells and once in terms of the benefit delivered, and explain what the second definition permits that the first forbids; (b) read the firm as a portfolio, placing each unit in a cell of the BCG matrix with a reason, and explain what the matrix cannot see about Hyperpure; (c) identify one growth option in each cell of the Ansoff grid for Blinkit specifically, and rank the four by risk with a justification; (d) conduct a situation analysis for the group, then name the one weakness-threat crossing that could do the most damage and the one strength-opportunity crossing you would fund first; (e) assess whether the firm's advantage in quick commerce is sustainable or temporary using the four tests, and say what a rival with unlimited capital could and could not copy within two years.","questions":[{"id":"module-1-q1","question":"A student is thirsty on a hot afternoon and buys a cold drink from the shop nearest the gate rather than the brand she prefers, which is sold two streets away. Which statement is correct?","options":["Her need was created by the drink's advertising","Her want was created by the marketer and her need was not","Her need existed independently, her want was shaped by exposure, and her demand was settled by convenience and price","She had a demand but no want, because she did not buy her preferred brand"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-1-q2","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the conditions necessary for an exchange to take place?","options":["Each party has something that might be of value to the other","A sale is concluded between the parties","Each party is free to accept or reject the other's offer","Each party believes it is appropriate to deal with the other"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q3","question":"Blinkit and Swiggy Instamart both promise groceries in about ten minutes from dark stores, advertise the same way and deliver equally well, yet one is profitable at the segment level and the other is not. The difference lies in which of the four jobs of marketing?","options":["Creating value","Communicating value","Delivering value","Capturing value"],"answer":3},{"id":"module-1-q4","question":"Zudio prices most of its range below one thousand rupees, spends almost nothing on advertising, designs in house and churns stock rapidly. This is best described as","options":["A pricing error compensated for by the other three Ps","An internally consistent marketing mix in which the other three Ps exist to make the price survivable","A promotion strategy, since low prices attract attention","A place strategy, since the stores are in high-footfall locations"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q5","question":"The four Ps of the marketing mix were originally proposed by","options":["Philip Kotler","E. Jerome McCarthy","Robert Lauterborn 15","Booms and Bitner"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q6","question":"A firm's product is genuinely good and priced competitively, yet sales are poor because buying it requires a long queue and a difficult return process. Under Lauterborn's framework, the problem is best diagnosed as","options":["Price, because the customer considers it expensive","Cost to the customer, which includes time, effort and risk beyond the price","Communication, because the benefits are not explained","Customer solution, because the product solves the wrong problem"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q7","question":"A firm runs its plant at full capacity year-round because idle capacity is the worst outcome a plant can have, and sells whatever comes off the line into whichever market will absorb it. This firm is running","options":["A market orientation","A societal marketing orientation","A production orientation","A niche strategy"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-1-q8","question":"Which statement best captures the difference between a sales orientation and a market orientation?","options":["A sales orientation spends more on promotion than a market orientation does","A sales orientation asks how to sell what the firm has, while a market orientation asks what the firm should have","A sales orientation serves consumers and a market orientation serves businesses","A market orientation avoids advertising altogether"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q9","question":"A cigarette company funds a large rural education programme and advertises the programme heavily. Applying the test given in these notes, this is","options":["A societal marketing orientation, because society benefits","Not a societal orientation, because the firm gave up nothing and the core product still harms long-run welfare","A market orientation, because it responds to what customers say they value","A production orientation, because it is funded from operating efficiency"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q10","question":"An energy drink marketed to students for late-night study sessions satisfies an immediate want and carries long- run health costs. In Kotler's classification it is","options":["A desirable product","A salutary product","A pleasing product","A deficient product"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-1-q11","question":"Which of the following is the correct order of the elements of a marketing plan?","options":["Marketing mix, mission, situation analysis, objectives, target market, implementation","Mission, situation analysis, objectives, target market strategy, marketing mix, implementation and control","Situation analysis, marketing mix, mission, objectives, implementation, target market","Objectives, mission, marketing mix, situation analysis, target market, control"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q12","question":"\"Increase market share and improve brand awareness among young customers by leveraging digital\" fails as a marketing objective mainly because","options":["It is too ambitious for the resources available","It names no segment, no baseline, no number and no date, so it cannot fail","It is inconsistent with the firm's mission","It uses digital channels rather than traditional ones"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q13","question":"A firm lists \"a growing Indian middle class\" among its strengths in a SWOT analysis. The error is that","options":["The statement is factually incorrect","It is an external factor available to every competitor, and therefore an opportunity rather than a strength","It is a threat rather than a strength","Strengths must always be financial"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q14","question":"In a TOWS analysis, the crossing at which firms most often actually fail is","options":["Strengths against opportunities","Strengths against threats","Weaknesses against opportunities","Weaknesses against threats 16"],"answer":3},{"id":"module-1-q15","question":"A firm holds twenty per cent of a market in which the largest competitor holds forty per cent. Its relative market share is","options":["0.5, placing it on the weak side of the BCG matrix","2.0, placing it on the strong side of the BCG matrix","20 per cent, which is above the dividing line","Not calculable without knowing the market growth rate"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-1-q16","question":"Which BCG cell describes a unit that leads a market growing at low single digits, needs little investment and generates surplus cash?","options":["Star","Question mark","Cash cow","Dog"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-1-q17","question":"Which of the following is NOT a valid criticism of the BCG matrix?","options":["It reduces market attractiveness to growth and competitive position to share","The way the market is defined determines which cell a unit falls into","It treats strategic business units as independent of one another","It cannot be applied to firms that have more than one business"],"answer":3},{"id":"module-1-q18","question":"Amul sells ice cream, cheese and chocolate to the same households that already buy its butter, using the same distribution route. In Ansoff's grid this is","options":["Market penetration","Product development","Market development","Diversification"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q19","question":"Diversification is treated as categorically riskier than the other three Ansoff strategies because","options":["It always requires more capital than the others","It involves two unknowns interacting, so a failure cannot be attributed and cannot be learned from","It is the only strategy that requires new employees","Regulators scrutinise it more closely"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-1-q20","question":"A retailer bought its store properties cheaply many years before the surrounding neighbourhoods developed. Applying the four tests of a sustainable advantage, this advantage is best described as","options":["Temporary, because any competitor with sufficient capital can buy property","Sustainable, because the advantage is made of time, which cannot be purchased at yesterday's prices","Valuable but not rare, since all retailers hold property","Rare but not exploitable, since property is a fixed asset"],"answer":1}]},{"slug":"module-2","label":"Module II","title":"Segmentation, targeting + positioning","filename":"Module2_Segmentation_Targeting_Positioning_Study_Notes.txt","application":"Application Question: Titan Titan Company sells watches, jewellery, eyewear, fragrances and ethnic wear. In watches alone it runs Zoop for children, Fastrack for the young, Sonata at the value end, Titan in the middle, Raga for women, and Nebula and Xylys at the top, alongside Titan Smart and Fastrack Smart in wearables. In jewellery it runs Tanishq for the mainstream buyer, Mia for the younger working woman, Zoya at the luxury end and CaratLane online. It also runs Titan EyePlus in eyewear, Skinn in fragrances and Taneira in sarees. Almost all of this is sold through a shared manufacturing base, a shared retail network and a shared corporate parent. Using the concepts in these notes, prepare an answer to the following. (a) Identify the bases of segmentation Titan uses in its watch business, naming the specific variable behind each brand, and say which brand pair is closest to being a single segment served twice; (b) test any two of Titan's smaller segments against the five requirements for effective segmentation, and state which requirement is hardest for them to satisfy; (c) classify Titan's targeting strategy using the four levels of market coverage, and explain what the firm gives up by choosing it rather than the alternatives; (d) draw a perceptual map of the Indian watch market on two dimensions you can defend, place Titan's brands and at least two competitors on it, and identify one gap worth entering and one that is empty for a good reason; (e) write a positioning statement in the standard form for Fastrack and one for Tanishq, and explain why the same firm can hold both positions without confusing either customer.","questions":[{"id":"module-2-q1","question":"Market segmentation is best defined as","options":["Dividing a market into distinct groups of buyers who have different needs, characteristics or behaviours and who might require separate marketing strategies or mixes","Selecting the most profitable customers in a market and ignoring the rest","Arranging for an offering to occupy a clear and distinctive place in the buyer's mind","Evaluating the attractiveness of each part of a market and choosing one to enter"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-2-q2","question":"A firm divides buyers of a commodity into people who own pets and people who do not, and finds that both groups buy identical quantities of the product in identical ways. This segmentation fails which requirement?","options":["Measurability","Accessibility","Differentiability","Substantiality"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-2-q3","question":"A small airline identifies seven distinct market segments but has too few staff to build a separate marketing programme for any of them. The segmentation fails on","options":["Substantiality","Actionability","Measurability","Accessibility"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q4","question":"A snack manufacturer sells different flavour mixes in Gujarat, West Bengal and Tamil Nadu. This is","options":["Psychographic segmentation","Behavioural segmentation","Geographic segmentation","Benefit segmentation"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-2-q5","question":"Titan sells Zoop to children, Fastrack to college students, Titan to working professionals and Nebula to the affluent buyer. The primary base being used is","options":["Occasion and usage rate","Age, life-cycle stage and income","Loyalty status","Product class 12"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q6","question":"Which statement about demographic segmentation is most accurate?","options":["It is the most popular base because demographic variables are easy to measure and often correlate with needs","It is the most accurate base because age reliably predicts life stage and buying power","It cannot be combined with other bases","It applies only to consumer markets and never to business markets"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-2-q7","question":"A coffee brand sells to buyers who care about the origin of the beans and the roast date, regardless of their age or income. This is","options":["Demographic segmentation","Geographic segmentation","Psychographic segmentation","Undifferentiated marketing"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-2-q8","question":"Cadbury Celebrations is built around Diwali and Raksha Bandhan rather than around a new chocolate. This is an example of","options":["Benefit segmentation","Occasion segmentation","Loyalty status segmentation","Income segmentation"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q9","question":"Colgate Strong Teeth, Sensodyne, Dabur Red and Closeup sell chemically similar products on completely different promises. The base being used is","options":["Benefits sought","User status","Usage rate","Geographic"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-2-q10","question":"A firm finds that twenty per cent of its customers account for most of its volume and concentrates its spending on them. It is segmenting on","options":["Loyalty status","Usage rate","Occasion","Life-cycle stage"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q11","question":"Which of the following is a segmentation variable used in business markets but not usually in consumer markets?","options":["Benefits sought","Usage rate","Purchasing approaches, such as whether buying is centralised or decentralised","Loyalty status"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-2-q12","question":"A luxury carmaker decides that its segment is affluent consumers everywhere rather than affluent countries, and begins selling into high-income groups within low-average-income markets. This is","options":["Geographic segmentation","Intermarket or cross-market segmentation","Undifferentiated marketing","Local marketing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q13","question":"In evaluating a market segment, a firm finds that it is large and fast-growing but already contains several aggressive well-funded competitors, entry is easy and buyers can switch at no cost. The firm is assessing","options":["Segment size and growth only","Structural attractiveness","Company objectives and resources","Segment measurability"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q14","question":"A luxury performance carmaker refuses to enter the large and growing economy car segment. This decision is best explained by","options":["The segment failing the size and growth test","The segment failing the structural attractiveness test","The segment not fitting the company's objectives and resources","The segment being unmeasurable 13"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-2-q15","question":"Hindustan Unilever sells Lifebuoy, Lux, Dove and Pears to different groups of soap buyers with separate products and separate campaigns. This market coverage strategy is","options":["Undifferentiated marketing","Differentiated marketing","Concentrated marketing","Individual marketing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q16","question":"Which is the main risk of a concentrated or niche strategy?","options":["It always costs more than differentiated marketing","The firm depends heavily on one segment, which may turn sour or be entered by a larger competitor","It cannot produce operating economies","It is unavailable to small firms"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q17","question":"A grocery app stocks each dark store according to what the surrounding two kilometres actually buys. This is best described as","options":["Local marketing","Individual marketing","Undifferentiated marketing","Intermarket segmentation"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-2-q18","question":"A perceptual map is most useful because it","options":["Reports each brand's actual market share","Shows how the target segment perceives competing brands on the dimensions buyers use, revealing gaps","Measures the profitability of each segment","Ranks brands by advertising expenditure"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-2-q19","question":"A hotel advertises itself as the tallest in the world. Most guests do not care. Which criterion for choosing a difference does this claim fail?","options":["Distinctive","Communicable","Important","Affordable"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-2-q20","question":"A brand changes its claim from value to aspiration to heritage across four years, and customers can no longer say what it stands for. This is","options":["Underpositioning","Overpositioning","Confused positioning","Doubtful positioning"],"answer":2}]},{"slug":"module-3","label":"Module III","title":"Marketing environment","filename":"Module3_Marketing_Environment_Study_Notes.txt","application":"Application Question: India's Real-Money Gaming Industry Between roughly 2018 and 2025, fantasy sports and other real-money gaming platforms built one of India's largest consumer internet categories. The demographic conditions were ideal: a median age near 29, cheap mobile data, and a national sport with a season that runs for months. The technological conditions were ideal too, because UPI made depositing money instant and free. The firms argued that their products were games of skill rather than games of chance, which placed them outside the state gambling prohibitions, and several state governments disagreed and were challenged in court. The industry bought heavy visibility during cricket, signed prominent endorsers and reached valuations in the billions of dollars. In August 2025, Parliament passed the Promotion and Regulation of Online Gaming Act, which prohibits online money gaming services and their advertising, while promoting e-sports and online social games. The business model ended in a week. Using the concepts in these notes, prepare an answer to the following. (a) Run a PESTEL analysis of this industry at its peak, taking each of the six letters in turn and stating whether it worked for or against the firms, then add the competitive factors the acronym leaves out; (b) map the microenvironment of one such firm, naming its suppliers, intermediaries, competitors and at least three of its publics, and say which public it under-managed; (c) explain what the industry attempted in the way of environmental management, and assess whether a different approach could have produced a different outcome; (d) using the socially responsible targeting discussion from Module II, argue whether the segmentation and targeting practised here was excellent marketing, exploitation, or both, and defend your position; (e) advise the management of a surviving e-sports or social-gaming firm on which environmental forces it should now scan most closely, and why.","questions":[{"id":"module-3-q1","question":"Which of the following belongs to a firm's microenvironment rather than its macroenvironment?","options":["The rate of inflation","The age structure of the population","The firm's marketing intermediaries","Technological change"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-3-q2","question":"A marketing manager states that the four Ps are controllable and the environment is not. The most accurate refinement of that statement is","options":["The macroenvironment can be controlled by large firms only","The microenvironment can be influenced but not controlled, and the macroenvironment can only be monitored and responded to","Both the micro and macro environments are fully controllable through public relations","Neither the marketing mix nor the environment is controllable"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q3","question":"The collection and interpretation of information about forces, events and relationships in the external environment that may affect the future of the organisation is called","options":["Environmental management","Environmental scanning","Market segmentation","A positioning audit"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q4","question":"A firm's finance department refuses to approve the price point that marketing has promised in its campaign. This is a problem in the","options":["Macroenvironment","Competitive environment","Internal environment","Natural environment"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-3-q5","question":"A consumer organisation publicly criticises a firm's advertising and the campaign is withdrawn, although sales had not fallen. The group that caused this is best described as 10","options":["A citizen-action public","A financial public","A reseller market","A supplier"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-3-q6","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of Kotler's five types of customer market?","options":["Consumer markets","Reseller markets","Government markets","Competitor markets"],"answer":3},{"id":"module-3-q7","question":"For a multiplex cinema, a streaming subscription is best classified as","options":["A brand competitor","A product competitor, because it is a different product form meeting the same need","A supplier","An intermediary"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q8","question":"Which statement about core and secondary cultural values is correct?","options":["Core values are highly persistent and secondary values are more open to change","Secondary values are highly persistent and core values change with each campaign","Both are equally easy for a marketer to change","Neither can be affected by marketing in any way"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-3-q9","question":"India's median age of roughly 29 years in 2026 is most directly a feature of which environment?","options":["Economic","Demographic","Political and legal","Natural"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q10","question":"Household sizes in Indian cities are falling as joint families separate. The most direct marketing consequence is that","options":["Total population growth accelerates","The number of households grows faster than the population, raising demand for items bought one per household","Demand for all products falls proportionately","Segmentation by income becomes impossible"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q11","question":"A period of high inflation typically causes consumers to","options":["Increase discretionary spending","Trade down to smaller packs, cheaper brands and delayed replacement","Become insensitive to price","Stop responding to any marketing activity"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q12","question":"The GST reform effective from 22 September 2025 replaced the earlier structure with two principal slabs of","options":["5 per cent and 12 per cent","5 per cent and 18 per cent, with a 40 per cent rate for a small set of demerit goods","12 per cent and 28 per cent","18 per cent and 28 per cent, with no other rates"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q13","question":"A firm finds that a tax reduction has lowered the cost of its category overnight. Which of the following is NOT one of its realistic options?","options":["Pass the reduction to the consumer as a lower price","Hold the price and take the additional margin","Hold the price and increase the pack size or grammage","Prevent competitors from receiving the same tax reduction"],"answer":3},{"id":"module-3-q14","question":"UPI removed the switching costs that payment wallets had built up. This illustrates that","options":["Technology only ever creates opportunities","A competitive advantage can be destroyed by a party that is not a competitor","Switching costs are always temporary by design","Wallets had no competitive advantage at any point 11"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q15","question":"Which of the following is a dark pattern named in the CCPA guidelines?","options":["Offering a genuine seasonal discount","Basket sneaking, where an item is added to the cart without the user's consent","Displaying customer reviews","Charging different prices in different cities"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q16","question":"The Promotion and Regulation of Online Gaming Act 2025","options":["Legalised all forms of online betting","Prohibited online money gaming services and their advertising while promoting e-sports and social games","Applied only to physical casinos","Required gaming firms to advertise only during cricket broadcasts"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q17","question":"A firm hires lobbyists, joins an industry association and runs advocacy advertising to shape the regulation of its category. This is","options":["Environmental scanning","Environmental management","Market targeting","Micromarketing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q18","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six letters of the PESTEL framework?","options":["Political","Economic","Competitive","Legal"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-3-q19","question":"A late monsoon reduces rural incomes, which reduces sales of two-wheelers and packaged goods within a quarter. This is an example of","options":["A microenvironmental actor affecting demand","An environmental force with immediate economic consequences","A political and legal force","A failure of internal marketing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-3-q20","question":"A ride-hailing platform enters food delivery using the rider fleet it already owns, surprising both incumbents. This best illustrates","options":["That industry boundaries are no longer clear and the dangerous entrant often comes from outside the industry","That brand-level competition is the only level worth tracking","That intermediaries always become competitors","That diversification carries no risk when a firm owns an asset"],"answer":0}]},{"slug":"module-4","label":"Module IV","title":"Consumer decision-making","filename":"Module4_Consumer_Decision_Making_Study_Notes.txt","application":"Application Question: A Student Buying a First Laptop Aarav is a second-year BBA student. His old laptop has slowed to the point where it struggles with the software his course now requires, and after a classmate turns up with a fast new machine, he decides he needs one too. He has a fixed budget from his parents, who want a say in the choice. Over two weeks he watches review videos, reads ratings on a shopping app, asks seniors, and visits one store to handle a few models. He narrows it to three brands and weighs price, battery life, weight and after-sales service. He nearly buys his top choice online, then a festival offer on a rival model and his father's preference for a brand with local service centres change his mind at the last moment. A week after the laptop arrives he feels a flicker of doubt about whether he should have waited for a newer model. Using the concepts in these notes, prepare an answer to the following. (a) Identify the type of buying behaviour Aarav is showing, and justify it using the two dimensions of involvement and brand difference; (b) trace Aarav through the five stages of the buyer decision process, naming what happens at each stage and what triggered the first one; (c) classify the information sources he used (personal, commercial, public, experiential) and explain which ones legitimised the product rather than merely informing him; (d) explain, using the purchase-decision stage, the two factors that came between his intention and his actual purchase; (e) name the feeling he has a week later, and advise the brand on one specific post-purchase action to reduce it.","questions":[{"id":"module-4-q1","question":"Consumer buyer behaviour refers to the buying behaviour of","options":["final consumers -- individuals and households buying for personal use","firms buying goods to resell at a profit","governments buying for public services","wholesalers buying in bulk"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q2","question":"In the model of buyer behaviour, the part the marketer cannot directly observe is called","options":["the buyer's black box","the marketing mix","the target market","the value proposition"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q3","question":"The four groups of factors that influence consumer behaviour are cultural, social, personal and","options":["psychological","political","technological","financial"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q4","question":"The most basic cause of a person's wants and behaviour, largely learned from family and society, is","options":["culture","income","personality","perception"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q5","question":"A group a person aspires to join and compares themselves to, even without being a member, is a","options":["reference group","membership group","social class","subculture"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q6","question":"A person within a group who, because of special knowledge or personality, influences others is an","options":["opinion leader","innovator 9","early adopter","intermediary"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q7","question":"The most important consumer buying organisation in society is the","options":["family","peer group","social class","workplace"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q8","question":"In Maslow's hierarchy, which need is the most basic, at the bottom of the pyramid?","options":["Physiological needs","Safety needs","Esteem needs","Self-actualisation needs"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q9","question":"A life-insurance advertisement that stresses protecting one's family appeals mainly to which Maslow need?","options":["Safety needs","Physiological needs","Esteem needs","Self-actualisation needs"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q10","question":"The tendency to notice, twist and remember information to fit what we already believe is part of","options":["perception","motivation","learning","adoption"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q11","question":"Buying an expensive product with high involvement and big differences between brands is","options":["complex buying behaviour","habitual buying behaviour","variety-seeking buying behaviour","dissonance-reducing buying behaviour"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q12","question":"Reaching for the same brand of salt every time out of habit rather than loyalty is","options":["habitual buying behaviour","complex buying behaviour","dissonance-reducing buying behaviour","variety-seeking buying behaviour"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q13","question":"Switching snack brands often just for a change, not because of dissatisfaction, is","options":["variety-seeking buying behaviour","habitual buying behaviour","complex buying behaviour","dissonance-reducing buying behaviour"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q14","question":"The correct order of the five stages of the buyer decision process is","options":["need recognition, information search, evaluation of alternatives, purchase decision, post-purchase behaviour","information search, need recognition, purchase, evaluation, post-purchase","need recognition, purchase decision, information search, evaluation, post-purchase","evaluation, information search, need recognition, purchase, post-purchase"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q15","question":"The buyer decision process begins with","options":["need recognition","information search","the purchase","post-purchase behaviour"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q16","question":"Which information source usually legitimises a product rather than merely informing the buyer?","options":["Personal sources such as friends and family","Commercial sources such as advertising 10","The product's own packaging","The manufacturer's website"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q17","question":"Two factors can come between purchase intention and the actual purchase. They are","options":["the attitudes of others and unexpected situational factors","price and promotion","culture and subculture","need recognition and information search"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q18","question":"The discomfort a buyer feels after an important purchase, caused by the drawbacks of the chosen brand, is","options":["cognitive dissonance","selective retention","need recognition","relative advantage"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q19","question":"The correct order of the five stages of the adoption process for a new product is","options":["awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption","interest, awareness, trial, evaluation, adoption","awareness, trial, interest, evaluation, adoption","evaluation, awareness, interest, trial, adoption"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-4-q20","question":"The first and most venturesome 2.5 per cent of buyers to adopt a new product are called","options":["innovators","early adopters","the early mainstream","lagging adopters"],"answer":0}]},{"slug":"module-5","label":"Module V","title":"Product + brand management","filename":"Module5_Product_and_Brand_Management_Study_Notes.txt","application":"Application Question: A New Protein Snack NutriBite is a mid-sized Indian food company known for biscuits and namkeen. It wants to launch a high-protein baked snack aimed at young urban gym-goers, a group it has not served before. Its research team screens fourteen ideas down to one, tests the concept with target consumers, builds a launch plan, checks the numbers, bakes and tests prototypes, sells the snack in two cities for three months, and then rolls it out nationally under a new sub-brand endorsed by the NutriBite name. A year after launch, sales are rising fast and three competitors have entered with similar snacks. Using the concepts in these notes, prepare an answer to the following. (a) Describe the new snack on the three levels of a product (core, actual, augmented), naming what sits at each level; (b) classify the snack as a consumer product type and justify the classification, then state which of the six categories of new products it is for NutriBite; (c) trace the snack through the eight steps of the new-product development process, matching each step to what NutriBite did; (d) identify which stage of the product life cycle the snack is in a year after launch, and recommend the marketing strategy appropriate to that stage; (e) evaluate NutriBite's branding decision to launch under a new sub-brand endorsed by the parent name, comparing it with a pure manufacturer's brand and a co-branding option.","questions":[{"id":"module-5-q1","question":"The most basic level of a product, which answers \"what is the buyer really buying?\", is the","options":["core customer value","actual product","augmented product","product line"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q2","question":"A warranty, after-sales service and delivery on credit are part of the","options":["augmented product","core customer value","actual product","product mix"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q3","question":"Detergent and snacks, bought frequently with little comparison, are","options":["convenience products","shopping products","specialty products","unsought products"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q4","question":"A luxury car that buyers will travel far to buy and rarely compare across brands is a","options":["specialty product","convenience product","shopping product","unsought product"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q5","question":"Life insurance, which people do not normally think of buying, is an example of a(n)","options":["unsought product","convenience product","shopping product","specialty product"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q6","question":"A single version of a product, such as one toothpaste in one size, is a","options":["product item","product line","product mix 8","product class"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q7","question":"A group of closely related product items is a","options":["product line","product item","product mix","brand"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q8","question":"The number of different product lines a company carries is the product mix's","options":["width","length","depth","consistency"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q9","question":"The number of versions of each product within a line is the product mix's","options":["depth","width","length","consistency"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q10","question":"Adding more items within the present range of a line is called","options":["line filling","line stretching","co-branding","commercialisation"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q11","question":"The differential effect that knowing the brand name has on a customer's response is","options":["brand equity","brand value","product quality","brand licensing"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q12","question":"When a large retailer sells a product under its own brand, it is a","options":["private (store) brand","manufacturer's brand","licensed brand","co-brand"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q13","question":"A store brand deliberately designed to look like an independent national brand, hiding the retailer's ownership, is a","options":["captive brand","manufacturer's brand","licensed brand","co-brand"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q14","question":"Two established brands from different firms placed together on one product is","options":["co-branding","line stretching","a private brand","licensing"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q15","question":"The first step in the new-product development process is","options":["idea generation","idea screening","business analysis","commercialisation"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q16","question":"Testing a new product and its full marketing programme in realistic market settings is","options":["test marketing","concept testing","business analysis 9","commercialisation"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q17","question":"The final step of the new-product development process, launching the product into the market, is","options":["commercialisation","test marketing","product development","idea screening"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q18","question":"A genuinely new invention that creates a market that did not exist before is a","options":["new-to-the-world product","repositioned product","cost-reduction product","line addition"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q19","question":"In which stage of the product life cycle are profits usually absent or negative because of heavy launch costs?","options":["Introduction","Growth","Maturity","Decline"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-5-q20","question":"Most products spend the largest part of their life in which PLC stage, defending market share?","options":["Maturity","Introduction","Growth","Decline"],"answer":0}]},{"slug":"module-6","label":"Module VI","title":"Marketing channels","filename":"Module6_Marketing_Channels_Study_Notes.txt","application":"Application Question A Bengaluru-based startup sells a premium cold-pressed juice. Today it sells only through its own website and delivers by courier across three cities. It wants to grow nationally over the next two years and is deciding how to distribute. Using the ideas in these notes, answer the following. (a) The founder says selling directly online means the startup has \"no channel and no intermediary costs.\" Explain why the channel functions still have to be performed, and identify three functions the startup is currently performing itself. (b) Draw and label the channel the startup uses today, and one longer channel it might use to reach kirana and modern-trade shelves nationally. State the level (zero, one or two) of each. (c) Recommend a distribution intensity (intensive, selective or exclusive) for this premium juice as it scales, and justify your choice against the nature of the product. (d) If the startup keeps its website while adding retail distribution, name the type of channel conflict most likely to arise, say between whom, and suggest one way to manage it. (e) The startup is offered a contract to become the licensed regional bottler-and-distributor for a large juice brand. Identify which kind of vertical marketing system this is, and give one advantage and one risk of accepting.","questions":[{"id":"module-6-q1","question":"A marketing channel is best defined as:","options":["A firm’s advertising and promotion plan","A set of interdependent organisations that help make a product available for use or consumption","The physical warehouse where goods are stored","The price list a producer gives to its dealers"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q2","question":"The main economic reason producers use intermediaries is that intermediaries:","options":["Always sell at a lower price than the producer","Reduce the number of contacts needed to reach the market","Remove the need to advertise","Eliminate all risk for the producer"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q3","question":"If a producer removes an intermediary from the channel, the functions that intermediary performed:","options":["Disappear along with it","Must be performed by some other member or by the producer itself","Are taken over automatically by the customer","Become unnecessary"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q4","question":"An intermediary that does NOT take ownership (title) of the goods is a:","options":["Merchant wholesaler","Retailer","Agent or broker","Distributor"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-6-q5","question":"Breaking the large lots a factory ships into the small quantities a household buys is called:","options":["Bulk-breaking","Financing","Promotion","Risk taking"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-6-q6","question":"Gathering products from many producers so buyers can find them together is the function of:","options":["Negotiation","Building an assortment","Coercion","Franchising"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q7","question":"A channel level is:","options":["The profit margin at each stage","A layer of intermediaries that performs work in bringing the product closer to the buyer","The number of products in a line","The distance goods travel"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q8","question":"A producer selling directly to consumers through its own website is using a:","options":["Three-level channel","Two-level channel","One-level channel","Zero-level (direct) channel"],"answer":3},{"id":"module-6-q9","question":"The standard channel for everyday convenience goods sold through thousands of small shops is typically a:","options":["Zero-level channel","Two-level channel (wholesaler and retailer)","Direct channel","Exclusive channel"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q10","question":"Compared with consumer channels, business (industrial) channels are usually:","options":["Longer, with more intermediaries","Shorter, because buyers are fewer and larger","Always exactly the same length","Never direct"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q11","question":"Stocking a product in as many outlets as possible is:","options":["Exclusive distribution","Selective distribution","Intensive distribution","Direct distribution"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-6-q12","question":"Giving a limited number of dealers the sole right to distribute in their territories is:","options":["Intensive distribution","Selective distribution","Exclusive distribution","Horizontal distribution"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-6-q13","question":"In a conventional distribution channel:","options":["One member owns all the others","Each member is a separate business seeking its own profit with little control over the rest","The members are bound by a franchising contract","There are never any intermediaries"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q14","question":"Producers, wholesalers and retailers acting as one unified system form a:","options":["Conventional channel","Vertical marketing system (VMS)","Horizontal marketing system","Zero-level channel"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q15","question":"Franchising is the most common form of a:","options":["Corporate VMS","Administered VMS","Contractual VMS","Horizontal marketing system"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-6-q16","question":"A VMS in which one company owns two or more levels of the channel is a:","options":["Contractual VMS","Administered VMS","Corporate VMS","Conventional channel"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-6-q17","question":"Two firms at the same level joining forces to pursue an opportunity neither could take alone is a:","options":["Vertical marketing system","Horizontal marketing system","Multichannel system","Corporate VMS"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q18","question":"The channel member that leads the others because it holds the most power is called the:","options":["Jobber","Channel captain","Broker","Franchisee"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q19","question":"A dispute between a producer and its retailers over margins is an example of:","options":["Horizontal conflict","Vertical conflict","Referent power","Bulk-breaking"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-6-q20","question":"The modern approach to channel management treats intermediaries as:","options":["Adversaries to be squeezed on price","Partners to be selected, motivated and managed for long-term value","A cost to be eliminated wherever possible","Temporary agents replaced each season"],"answer":1}]},{"slug":"module-7","label":"Module VII","title":"Pricing concepts","filename":"Module7_Pricing_Concepts_Study_Notes.txt","application":"Application Question A Bengaluru startup is launching a premium oat-milk carton aimed at health-conscious Gen Z buyers. Each carton costs Rs 40 to make in variable cost, the startup carries Rs 8,00,000 in fixed cost a year, and it is considering a launch price of Rs 120. Established dairy milk sells at about Rs 30 and one imported oat-milk brand sells at Rs 150. Using the ideas in these notes, answer the following. (a) Identify the price floor and the price ceiling for this product, and explain what sets each. Where do the two competitor prices sit relative to that band? (b) Using the break-even formula, calculate how many cartons the startup must sell in a year at Rs 120 just to break even. Show the contribution per carton. (c) The founder considers dropping the launch price to Rs 90 to attract more buyers. Explain what happens to the break-even volume, and state the risk this creates. (d) Recommend whether the startup should use market-skimming or market-penetration pricing at launch, and justify your choice against the conditions each strategy requires. (e) Predict how the price of this product is likely to move as the category grows and more oat-milk brands enter, and name the life-cycle stage where price pressure will be greatest.","questions":[{"id":"module-7-q1","question":"Price is best described as:","options":["The cost of making a product","The sum of the values customers give up to gain the benefits of a product","The profit a firm earns","The amount a firm spends on promotion"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q2","question":"Among the four Ps, price is unique because it is the only element that:","options":["Costs the firm money","Cannot be changed once set","Produces revenue rather than representing a cost","Is decided by the customer"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-7-q3","question":"Price is also described as the most ____ element of the marketing mix.","options":["Expensive","Flexible (it can be changed quickly)","Permanent","Ignored"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q4","question":"Setting a low price simply to keep the business running during overcapacity or heavy competition is the objective of:","options":["Profit maximisation","Product-quality leadership","Survival","Status quo"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-7-q5","question":"Pricing high to signal and support superior quality reflects the objective of:","options":["Market-share leadership","Product-quality leadership","Survival","Penetration"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q6","question":"In the three Cs, costs set the:","options":["Price ceiling","Price floor","Competitor price","Discount level"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q7","question":"In the three Cs, customer perceptions of value set the:","options":["Price floor","Price ceiling","Break-even point","Mark-up"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q8","question":"Adding a standard mark-up to the unit cost of a product is:","options":["Value-based pricing","Cost-plus (cost-based) pricing","Penetration pricing","Dynamic pricing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q9","question":"The main weakness of pure cost-based pricing is that it:","options":["Is difficult to calculate","Ignores customer value and demand","Always sets prices too low","Cannot be used by large firms"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q10","question":"The break-even volume is calculated as:","options":["Fixed cost × price","Fixed cost ÷ (price − variable cost per unit)","Variable cost ÷ price","Price − total cost"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q11","question":"If a firm cuts its price while costs stay the same, the break-even volume:","options":["Falls","Rises","Stays exactly the same","Becomes zero"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q12","question":"Setting the price from customers’ perceptions of value, with cost only as a floor, is:","options":["Cost-plus pricing","Value-based pricing","Going-rate pricing","Break-even pricing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q13","question":"Following the market leader’s price is an example of:","options":["Value-added pricing","Going-rate (competition-based) pricing","Skimming","Segmented pricing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q14","question":"Launching a new product at a high price to earn maximum revenue from segments willing to pay most is:","options":["Market-penetration pricing","Market-skimming pricing","Good-value pricing","Promotional pricing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q15","question":"Launching at a low price to win a large market share quickly is:","options":["Market-skimming pricing","Market-penetration pricing","Product-quality leadership","Psychological pricing"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q16","question":"Market-penetration pricing works best when:","options":["The market is price-sensitive and higher volume lowers cost","The product is unique and buyers will pay a premium","Competitors cannot enter the market","The firm wants to signal high quality"],"answer":0},{"id":"module-7-q17","question":"Across the product life cycle, price competition is usually most intense during:","options":["Introduction","Growth","Maturity","Product development"],"answer":2},{"id":"module-7-q18","question":"As the number of similar competitors rises, a firm’s freedom to set price on its own generally:","options":["Increases","Decreases","Stays unchanged","Disappears completely and price becomes zero"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q19","question":"Temporary price cuts such as festival discounts and cashbacks are:","options":["Value-added pricing","Promotional pricing","Cost-plus pricing","Skimming"],"answer":1},{"id":"module-7-q20","question":"Pricing a product at Rs 499 rather than Rs 500 is an example of:","options":["Dynamic pricing","Psychological pricing","Penetration pricing","Break-even pricing"],"answer":1}]}];